diagnosis_text:
The LAG (bond) between sshswitch and router17 is misconfigured on both ends. Both bond0 interfaces are running in **balance-rr (round-robin, mode 0)** with **MII monitoring disabled (miimon=0)** and no ARP monitoring (arp_interval=0). The correct mode for this two-link aggregate should be **active-backup (mode 1)** with a non-zero miimon (e.g., 100 ms).

**Mechanism:** Round-robin mode distributes successive outgoing frames alternately between eth6 and eth7 on sshswitch, and between eth0 and eth1 on router17. Because these two slaves traverse separate Kathara collision domains (r17_ms1 and r17_ms1_2) with independent packet-processing queues, frames within a single TCP flow arrive at the far end out of sequence. The TCP receiver issues duplicate ACKs, triggering fast-retransmit and, when that fails, retransmission-timeout back-off (initial RTO ~200 ms, doubling on each miss). Cascading RTOs produce multi-second application stalls that "come back on their own" once TCP succeeds — exactly the "pauses for a few seconds" reported by the users. With miimon=0, the kernel bond driver has no mechanism to detect a failed slave and pull it from rotation; if either virtual link experiences a transient glitch, 50% of frames are silently discarded until the condition clears, compounding the packet-loss effect.

**Localization:** Both PC1 and PC2 are on VLAN5 (10.5.0.0/24), gateway sshswitch:br_v5 (10.5.0.254). All traffic leaving that VLAN toward remote subnets exits sshswitch via bond0 over the faulty link to router17. The shared bond is why both users freeze simultaneously.

**Root cause:** Both bond0 interfaces (sshswitch and router17) were set to balance-rr with no link monitoring, causing continuous TCP-flow packet reordering across the two-slave round-robin.

**Fix:** On both sshswitch and router17, change bond mode to active-backup and enable MII monitoring:
`echo active-backup > /sys/class/net/bond0/bonding/mode; echo 100 > /sys/class/net/bond0/bonding/miimon`
=== END ===